<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Brno</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Brünn</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Brno is the second-largest city of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czech-republic">Czech Republic</a> and the historic capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moravia">Moravia</a>. A Přemyslid castle stood there by the 11th century, when it became the seat of an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/appanage">appanage</a> duchy, and the town received its privileges in 1243. Its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spilberk-castle">Špilberk Castle</a> later served as a notorious <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> prison, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gregor-mendel">Gregor Mendel</a> carried out his experiments on heredity at the city's Augustinian abbey.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/brno.jpg" alt="Brno" width="320" height="573" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Doronenko · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brno_Montage_IV.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Brno lies at the confluence of the Svitava and Svratka rivers, between the Bohemian-Moravian Highlands and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thaya">Dyje</a>–Svratka Valley in the south-east of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czech-republic">Czech Republic</a>. It stands at the crossroads of ancient trade routes linking northern and southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and has always been closely tied to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a>, 110 km to the south. The etymology of the name is disputed: it may derive from Old Czech brnie, muddy or swampy, from the Slavic verb brniti, to armour or fortify, or from a Celtic word related to Welsh bryn, hill. It was known in German as Brünn and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> as Bruna.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The city's direct precursor was Staré Zámky, a fortified hillfort of Great Moravia near Brno-Líšeň, inhabited from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neolithic">Neolithic</a> until the early 11th century. In the early 11th century Brno became the seat of a non-ruling Přemyslid prince and one of the centres of Moravia, along with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olomouc">Olomouc</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/znojmo">Znojmo</a>. It is first mentioned in Cosmas's Chronica Boemorum in 1091, when King Vratislaus II besieged his brother Conrad in Brno castle. From the late 12th century until the middle of the 17th, political power in the Margraviate of Moravia was divided between Brno and Olomouc, which was the larger and the seat of the only bishopric in Moravia until 1777.</p><p>In 1641 Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferdinand-iii-holy-roman-emperor">Ferdinand III</a>, as margrave of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moravia">Moravia</a>, moved the Moravian Diet, Land Court and Land Tables from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olomouc">Olomouc</a> to Brno, since Swedish armies had made Olomouc their primary target in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirty-years-war">Thirty Years' War</a>. Brno, under Jean-Louis Raduit de Souches, resisted the siege of 1645 by Field Marshal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lennart-torstensson">Lennart Torstensson</a>, the only Moravian city to defend itself against the Swedes, and it then served as the sole capital of Moravia. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-austerlitz">Battle of Austerlitz</a> in December 1805 the French occupied it, and in 1809 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> ordered the fortifications of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spilberk-castle">Špilberk Castle</a> dismantled.</p><p>At the start of the 20th century about two thirds of Brno's residents spoke German, though the suburbs were mostly Czech-speaking, and everyday life was bilingual. During the German occupation of 1939 to 1945 the Nazis closed the Czech universities and used the Kounic Residence Hall as a political prison, where about 800 civilians were executed or died. After the communist coup of 1948 the regime abolished the traditional regional governance, ending Brno's role as the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moravia">Moravia</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Brno is the second-largest city of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czech-republic">Czech Republic</a> and the political and cultural hub of the South Moravian Region. Its traditional symbols are <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spilberk-castle">Špilberk Castle</a> and the Cathedral of Saints Peter and Paul on Petrov hill. The Villa Tugendhat, a functionalist building, was added to the UNESCO World Heritage List in 2001. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gregor-mendel">Gregor Mendel</a> lived and died in the city.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Brno” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Gregor Mendel, an Augustinian friar, presented his pea-plant experiments on heredity in <b>Brünn</b>, Moravia, in 1865; they were largely ignored until they were rediscovered in 1900.</blockquote><a href="/empire/austrian-empire">Austrian Empire · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>26; its name joins <b>Brno</b>, where it was designed, and Enfield, where it was made.</blockquote><a href="/empire/czechoslovakia">Czechoslovakia · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>After that state's collapse it passed, probably in the early eleventh century, under the Přemyslid dukes of Bohemia, who divided it into appanage principalities centred on Olomouc, <b>Brno</b> and Znojmo and granted them to junior members of the dynasty.</blockquote><a href="/empire/margraviate-of-moravia">Margraviate of Moravia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>After his death in 1055, his sons divided it; Conrad I received the <b>Brno</b> portion, centred on the castle of Brno (Brünn) in southern Moravia, while Olomouc and later Znojmo formed separate appanages.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-brno">Duchy of Brno</a></li><li><blockquote>Znojmo in southern Moravia was split off from the <b>Brno</b> appanage around 1092, when Conrad I of Brno became Duke of Bohemia and his son Litold received Znojmo.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-znojmo">Duchy of Znojmo</a></li></ul></section>
